WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.370
NARRATOR: A fruit turnover
is probably your best

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.200
bet if you're eating on the go.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.180
It satisfies a sweet
tooth, it's portable,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.270
and it requires no utensils.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.600
The dough, typically
flaky, is folded over,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.210
usually into a
triangle or rectangle,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.490
and the edges are sealed,
trapping the fruit

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.645
filling neatly inside.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.210
These all natural blueberry
turnovers may look and taste

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.590
like homemade, but believe it or
not, they come frozen in a box.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.230
You just bake them and eat them.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.560
The manufacturer is willing
to reveal the ingredients.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.680
But the critical proportions,
mixing and heating times

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.660
are top secret.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.960
Workers begin making the
filling by heating blueberries.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.630
Next, they combine lemon juice
and starch in a container.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.330
Then, add this mixture
to the blueberries.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.489
The starch is
white now, but will

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:06.335
become transparent as it cooks.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.425
They mix some more.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:25.170
Then add salt. When the starch
is opaque, they add sugar

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.010
and mix some more.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.430
As soon as the temperature
of the ingredients

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.895
reaches 170 degrees Fahrenheit,
the filling is done.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.070
To make the dough, workers
first pour flour into the mixer.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:50.830
Then salt. After a
few minutes of mixing,

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.885
they add butter, lots of butter.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.900
Once it's well blended,
they add some water.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.410
After a bit more mixing,
the dough is ready.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:06.010
This type of dough is called
pate au beurre, the French term

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.139
for a butter-based
pastry that's extremely

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.575
light and flaky due to its
high ratio of fat to flour.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.960
Now, workers weigh
out 12 pound portions

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.220
and put them one at a time into
a dough press with a sprinkling

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.690
of flour to prevent sticking.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.310
The press compresses
the dough into a 10

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.160
by 16 inch block
that's one inch thick.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.440
Then they line up the blocks
on a floured conveyor belt,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.470
joining them together to form
one continuous sheet of dough.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.770
The dough sheet
now enters a press.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.590
Inside, rollers compress
the sheet to about a quarter

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.150
of its original thickness.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.215
The next machine layers
the thin dough sheet.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.370
This flower press and layer
process repeats over and over

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.370
again until the sheet is
just a tenth of an inch

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.010
thick, yet contains 24 layers.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.370
These layers are what
will make the pastry

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:16.975
exceptionally light and flaky.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.340
Trimming wheel's on the
sides make a neat edge.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.965
Diagonally bladed wheels score
the dough with small cuts.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:30.280
And cutting wheel's
divide the sheet

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.150
into five strips, each of which
will become a row of turnovers.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.110
The next station deposits
a line of water droplets

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.810
along one edge of each strip.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.715
This creates a line
of sticky dough.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:47.710
The preparations are all
done and assembly can begin.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.440
First, with precision
spacing, nozzles

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.825
squirt one ounce of blueberry
filling per turnover.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.450
Then a device called a
folding plow, because it looks

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.360
like a farm plow, lifts
one edge of the dough

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.815
and gradually folds it over.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.940
A smooth wheel then presses that
edge down onto the sticky line

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.620
on the other edge.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.615
This seals the open
side of the strips.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.730
The culinary choreography
ends with a bang, or rather,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.970
a chop, as a guillotine
descends cutting the strips

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.780
into individual turnovers,
and simultaneously

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.075
sealing the front and
back ends of each pastry.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.180
The assembled turnovers
now go on a 6-minute ride

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.610
through a nitrogen
flash freezing tunnel.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.180
Temperature, minus 183
degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.480
Under a shower of
sugar crystals,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.985
they exit the tunnel,
frozen, hard as a rock.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.910
Packaged, four to
a box the turnovers

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.400
remain frozen until you
bake them in your oven

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.640
and serve them up.

